Here is a simple Rust function that solves the problem. This function assumes that the input is a JSON array of arrays, where each inner array has two elements. Each inner array represents a job with an arrival time and a duration. The function calculates the total waiting time for each job.

```rust
use serde_json::Value;

pub fn solve(input: Value) -> Value {
    let jobs: Vec<Vec<i64>> = input.as_array().unwrap();
    let mut waiting_times: Vec<i64> = Vec::new();
    let mut current_time = 0;

    for job in jobs {
        let arrival_time = job[0];
        let duration = job[1];

        if arrival_time > current_time {
            current_time = arrival_time;
        }

        waiting_times.push(current_time - arrival_time);
        current_time += duration;
    }

    Value::from(waiting_times)
}
```

This function works by iterating over the jobs in the order they arrive. For each job, it calculates the waiting time as the difference between the current time and the arrival time of the job. If the current time is less than the arrival time, it sets the current time to the arrival time. Then, it adds the duration of the job to the current time.

The function returns a JSON array of waiting times for each job.
